of all systems to be called.  _U_u_c_i_c_o  will  then  call  each

system and process all _w_o_r_k _f_i_l_e_s.


_C_a_l_l _R_e_m_o_t_e _S_y_s_t_e_m


The call is made using information from several files  which

reside  in  the uucp program directory.  At the start of the

call process, a lock is set to forbid multiple conversations

between the same two systems.


The system name is found in the _L._s_y_s file.  The information

contained for each system is;


     [1]  system name,


     [2]  times to call the system (days-of-week and  times-

          of-day),










                                                          17


     [3]  device or device type to be used for call,


     [4]  line speed,


     [5]  datawidth.  Normally _8_f_N for modem or direct lines

          but is set (for example to _7_f_N) when communicating

          over a network or _8_f_Y if the fio protocol is to be

          used.


     [6]  phone number if field [3] is  _A_C_U  or  the  device

          name (same as field [3]) if not _A_C_U,


     [7]  login information (multiple fields),


The time field is checked against the present time to see if

the call should be made.


The _p_h_o_n_e _n_u_m_b_e_r may contain  abbreviations  (e.g.  mh,  py,

boston)  which  get translated into dial sequences using the

_L-_d_i_a_l_c_o_d_e_s file.


The _L-_d_e_v_i_c_e_s file is scanned using fields [3] and [4]  (and

for  direct lines [6]) from the _L._s_y_s file to find an avail-

able device for the call.  The program will try all  devices

which satisfy [3] and [4] until the call is made, or no more

devices can be tried.  If a device is successfully opened, a

lock file is created so that another copy of _u_u_c_i_c_o will not

