_L._s_y_s


Each entry in this file represents one system which  can  be

called  by  the  local  uucp  programs.   Each entry has, at

least, seven fields:


            site time caller speed width phone login


These are described below.


     _s_i_t_e


     The name of the remote system.


     _t_i_m_e


     This is a string which indicates the  days-of-week  and

     times-of-day  when  the  system  should be called (e.g.

     MoTuTh0800-1730).


     The day portion may be a list containing some of


          _S_u _M_o _T_u _W_e _T_h _F_r _S_a


     or it may be _W_k for any week-day or _A_n_y for any day.


     The time should be a range of times  (e.g.  0800-1230).

     If  no  time  portion  is specified, any time of day is

     assumed to be ok for the call.


     Several possible choices may  be  given  by  seperating

     them with a '|' (e.g. Mo0800-1730|Tu0000-0600).












                                                          41


     _c_a_l_l_e_r


     This is normally either _A_C_U or _D_I_R but other  type  are

     availble for specialised networks.


     _s_p_e_e_d


     This is the line speed for the call (e.g. 300).


     _w_i_d_t_h


     This  is  effectively  the  number  of  bits  that  the

     transfer  protocol  will  use.   Normally it it _8_f_N for

     ACU's and most DIR's.  However if the line is  attached

     to  a network that does not provide full eight bit data

     transfers (such as PSS or JANET, depending on your con-

     nection)  then  this  can  be set to _7 _6 _4 Where _7 will

     work reasonably efficiently  over  most  most  networks

     that  can  provide interactive terminal work.  _4 is for

     relatively brain-damaged networks and although  it  can

     transfer in the face of great adversity it does so very

     slowly.  _6 is a variant of _7.  All must have the trail-

     ing _f_N.


     Over some networks the default packet and window  sizes

     selected  by  the  _u_u_c_i_c_o  packet  driver  may  not  be

     optimal.   These  can  be  changed  by   adding   colon

     seperated  fields  to  the end of the width field.  For

     example _7:_2:_1_2_8 will select a data width of _7, a window

     size  of _2 windows and a packet size of _1_2_8 characters.










                                                          42


     The defaults for these are a packet size of  sixty-four

     characters and a window size of three.


     It has been found that for PSS and  JANET  setting  the

     width  field  to  _7:_2 (default packet size and two win-

     dows) is often best.


     When the FIO protocol is enabled (it is a compile  time

     option)  it is normally necessary to disable it on sin-

     gle lines.  To do this add the string "fN" to  the  end

     of the width entry. E.g: _8_f_N It should always be at the

     end to allow the window and packet fields to be parsed.


     _p_h_o_n_e


     The phone number is made up of an  optional  alphabetic

     abbreviation  and  a numeric part.  The abbreviation is

     one which appears in the _L-_d_i_a_l_c_o_d_e_s  file  (e.g.  lon-

     don1237654, kent2223456).


